      <workList xml:id="workDesc_75b0974d">
         <work xml:id="work_idddfbc1d7">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_b6714423">C4:007</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_idf9851ded" xml:lang="de">Lustspiele der Jungfer Charlotta Dorothea Biehl</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_bf7eeb10">
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_id54166a8b">Charlotta Dorothea Biehl</persName>
               <persName role="translator" xml:id="persName_3ffd7a60">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id421597ee">
               <date isodate="1767" xml:id="date_22ca74f2">1767</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id49c32314">
               <p xml:id="idm94">Scheibe only translated a few plays of which the present volume appearing in 1767 consists of two parts; it includes in all six plays or comedies. The playwright is <persName xml:id="idm95" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Charlotta Dorothea Biehl</persName> who herself was a translator. In his <rend xml:id="idm96" fontstyle="italic">Anekdoten</rend> (1771; SchW C3:001) Scheibe mentions that the Danes indeed have some poets and historians who do them honour; however, there are also some learned women ('lærde Fruentimmer') who should be mentioned: ‘Andersen, Biel, Beck’. Scheibe has included the usual foreword briefly mentioning when the comedies were written, staged and translated. Thus the translation of the first comedy, ‘Der zärtliche Ehemann’ (‘Den kierlige Mand’) was finished in the beginning of 1765 – for the present edition, the translation had been revised. The second comedy, ‘Der Sylbenstecher’ (‘Haarkløveren’), was staged shortly after the first one and was printed in 1765; Scheibe explains issues concerning translating satire from Danish to German. The third one, ‘Die listige Betrügerinn’ (‘Den listige Optrækkerske’), was staged in autumn 1765 and was published at that time. Also the fourth, 'Der verliebte Freund' ('Den forelskede Ven'), appeared in autumn 1765; and the fifth, 'Die zärtliche Tochter' ('Den kiærlige Datter'), which was also issued in 1765 received a prize for being the best comedy of the year. In 1766 the theatre was closed due the king's death and the piece had therefore not been staged. The final comedy in the volume is ‘Der Zwist’ (‘Tvistigheden eller Critique over den listige Optrækkerske’). A very brief review appeared in <rend xml:id="idm97" fontstyle="italic">Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek</rend> (appendix to 1765) mentioning that the work was not necessary to include in a German library though ‘the translation has not turned out bad’ (‘Die Uebersetzung ist nicht übel gerathen’). Though the name of the translator does not appear on the title page, Scheibe's name is mentioned in the Danish edition of the works which appeared in 1773. Here it is told that Scheibe, immediately following the success of the first comedy in October 1764, produced the German translation; this is also implied in the article regarding theatrical news as well as on opera from Copenhagen which appeared in <rend xml:id="idm98" fontstyle="italic">Bibliothek der schönen Wissenschaften</rend> (1765). However, the review in <rend xml:id="idm99" fontstyle="italic">Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek</rend> indicated that not all the translations were satisfactory. Apparently, the translated comedies were also reviewed in English journals.</p>
            </history>
